+# Decision: How committed changes reach browsers
+
+## Context
+
+MarketBoard keeps one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS and the server assigns each committed edit one event revision. This decision covers the other direction: how a committed revision gets from the server to every browser showing that event.
+
+The load is modest but bursty. Up to 800 staff browsers are connected at once, a busy market has 10-25 editors averaging two status changes per minute, and opening time brings bursts of 20 edits per second. The project goals require committed changes to appear within 15 seconds under normal connectivity, a browser to recover the latest board after up to 20 minutes disconnected, and 99.5 percent availability during published event hours.
+
+The choice shapes three components at once. The change store's history and publication duties, the delivery component's runtime, and the browser's connection logic all take a different form under each option, so they cannot be designed until it is made. Reversing it later means reworking all three.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+No gate separates the options. All three meet the 15-second freshness goal, the 20-minute recovery goal, and the 800-browser scale, and the tenets permit both self-hosted and managed infrastructure because event updates carry stall IDs and operational statuses, not customer records. The decision is made on weighted criteria.
+
+- **Update latency — major.** The delay between an edit committing and other browsers showing it. Coordinators use the board to act on closures, late arrivals, and power problems while they are happening, and several people act on the same board at once. Latency below the 15-second goal is not required, but it is the main thing this decision can buy.
+- **Provider independence — major.** Whether the board's delivery path, and the browser code written against it, depends on one vendor's product and subscription model. No tenet sets this weight; it is specific to this decision. Delivery is the part of the system every browser touches continuously, so a dependency here is the hardest one to remove later.
+- **Failure visibility — major.** How directly a broken delivery path shows up as a failure in the browser. Importance derives from the tenet *Connectivity failures must be visible, not mistaken for an unchanged board.*
+- **Build and operations burden — moderate.** What the team must build, run, and debug itself: connection capacity, deploy behavior, reconnect storms, replay.
+- **Recurring cost — minor.** Ongoing spend attributable to delivery, whether a vendor fee or load on MarketBoard's own servers.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-second HTTPS polling
+
+Each browser asks the server every five seconds for revisions after the last one it has. The server answers from the change store with a cursor query and holds no per-browser state between requests. Delivery stays ordinary request/response, so it scales, deploys, and fails like the rest of the HTTPS API. A browser that was offline simply resumes polling from its cursor; catch-up and normal operation are the same code path.
+
+- Update latency: **weak**. Every change waits for the next poll, so lag is up to five seconds plus the request, on every edit.
+- Provider independence: **strong**. Uses only the project's own HTTPS API.
+- Failure visibility: **strong**. A failed or late poll is an explicit error in the browser; there is no connection that can look alive while delivering nothing.
+- Build and operations burden: **strong**. No sessions, no reconnect logic, no publication path; the store needs cursor queries and the browser needs a timer loop.
+- Recurring cost: **partial**. No vendor fee, but 800 browsers produce 160 reads per second whether or not anything changed.
+
+Summary: the least to build and run, at the price of a fixed few seconds of lag.
+
+### Self-hosted WebSockets
+
+Each browser holds a WebSocket to a delivery component that MarketBoard runs. When an edit commits, the change store publishes the revision to delivery, which pushes it to every socket viewing that event. Because a socket can drop at any moment, the store keeps a replayable revision history and a reconnecting browser asks for everything after the last revision it applied.
+
+- Update latency: **strong**. Revisions are pushed as they commit.
+- Provider independence: **strong**. The protocol is a web standard and every part of the path is the project's own.
+- Failure visibility: **partial**. A dropped socket is visible, but a half-open one can look connected while delivering nothing, and a revision lost between the store and delivery is invisible to the browser. Both need deliberate detection.
+- Build and operations burden: **weak**. The team owns socket sessions, capacity for 800 long-lived connections, connection draining on deploy, reconnect behavior after an outage, reliable publication, and replay.
+- Recurring cost: **strong**. No vendor fee, and idle browsers cost an open connection, not repeated reads.
+
+Summary: fast and fully owned, paid for in connection and reconnect work.
+
+### Hosted realtime service
+
+The server publishes each committed revision to a managed realtime provider, and browsers subscribe through the provider's client library. The provider runs the connections, handles fan-out, and manages reconnects. MarketBoard's delivery component becomes a thin wrapper that publishes to the provider and issues subscription credentials, plus a reconciliation step that detects revisions the provider never received.
+
+- Update latency: **strong**. Low-latency push, available soonest of the three because the connection layer already exists.
+- Provider independence: **weak**. Browser subscription code, credentials, and the publication path are written against one provider's model, and delivery availability during event hours depends on that provider.
+- Failure visibility: **partial**. The provider's client reports connection state, but a failed publication from the server to the provider leaves browsers connected and unaware; reconciliation has to catch it.
+- Build and operations burden: **partial**. No connection operations, but publication reconciliation and provider integration remain.
+- Recurring cost: **weak**. A recurring provider fee for as long as the board runs.
+
+Summary: fast with little connection work, in exchange for a standing fee and a dependency on one provider.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Self-hosted WebSockets.**
+
+It is the only option that is strong on both update latency and provider independence. Polling gives up latency to keep independence; a hosted service gives up independence to keep latency. Self-hosting keeps both and pays in build and operations burden, where it is the weakest option. That trade is accepted deliberately: the burden is weighted below the two criteria it buys, the scale is 800 connections in a single region, and the work is the project's own to learn and tune.
+
+The decision does not turn on a project tenet. Provider independence is weighted major for this decision alone; the tenets still permit managed infrastructure elsewhere. It is taken with one tenet in tension: on failure visibility, polling is the stronger option, and choosing sockets means the visibility the tenet requires has to be built instead of inherited.
+
+Committing to it obligates:
+
+- **Change store:** a replayable revision history and reliable publication of every committed revision to delivery, so a missed push is recoverable and never silent.
+- **Delivery:** socket session management, capacity for the full browser population, and connection handling across deploys and restarts.
+- **Browser sync:** detection of dead and half-open connections, a visible disconnected state, reconnect, and catch-up from the last applied revision, covering the 20-minute recovery goal.
+
+It forecloses stateless delivery: availability during event hours now depends on long-lived connections the project runs.
+
+The judgment is worth re-running if either major weight changes. If updates within a few seconds turn out to be as good as immediate for coordinators, polling wins on every remaining criterion. If the connection work proves more than the team can sustain and a provider dependency becomes acceptable, the hosted service delivers the same latency for less effort.
