 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets deliver committed changes.** MarketBoard runs its own socket endpoint and pushes each revision to connected browsers as it commits. All three candidates meet the 15-second freshness goal, so the choice turns on what is paid for updates faster than the goal requires. Five-second HTTPS polling was rejected: it is the simplest to operate and stateless, but it leaves a few seconds of lag on every change and repeats reads from up to 800 browsers whether or not anything changed. A hosted realtime service was rejected: it gives low latency with the least connection operations work, but adds a recurring fee and couples the board to one provider's subscription model. Self-hosting gets the fast updates without that dependency; the accepted cost is owning connection capacity, reconnect handling, and replay. The options and criteria are laid out in [docs/decisions/change-delivery.md](decisions/change-delivery.md).
